      <h1>How to Handle an Estate Sale in Texas</h1>
      <p class="hero-sub">Clearing out a loved one's home is one of the harder parts of settling an estate. Here's how estate sales work, what your options are, and how to make the process as smooth as possible.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container article-layout">
      <article class="article-body">

        <div class="article-intro">
          <p>After a loved one passes away, one of the most immediate practical challenges families face is figuring out what to do with everything inside the home. Furniture, clothing, collectibles, tools, jewelry, vehicles, decades of accumulated belongings. It can feel overwhelming, especially when you're also navigating grief, probate, and family coordination at the same time.</p>
          <p>An estate sale is one of the most common solutions. This guide covers how the process works, what your options are, and what to watch out for so you can make a confident decision for your family.</p>
        </div>

        <h2>What Is an Estate Sale?</h2>
        <p>An estate sale is a sale of most or all of the personal property inside a home, typically held on-site over one to three days. Unlike a garage sale, estate sales are usually professionally organized and priced, attract serious buyers, and can move a significant volume of items in a short period of time.</p>
        <p>Estate sales are common after a death, but also after a divorce, a move to assisted living, or any situation where someone needs to liquidate a household quickly. For families dealing with an inherited property, they're often the most practical way to clear the home before selling it.</p>

        <h2>Estate Sale vs. Other Options</h2>
        <p>Before committing to an estate sale, it's worth understanding the other paths and what each one involves.</p>

        <div class="qa-list">
          <div class="qa-item">
            <h4>Professional estate sale company</h4>
            <p>A company comes in, catalogs and prices everything, markets the sale, runs it over a weekend, and handles the transactions. They typically take a commission of 25 to 40 percent of gross sales. You do very little work and get a cleared home at the end. This is the most common choice for larger estates or families who don't have the time or bandwidth to manage it themselves.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-item">
            <h4>Online auction or consignment</h4>
            <p>Companies like Everything But The House or local auction houses will list items individually online or at auction. This can yield higher prices for valuable items but takes longer and doesn't clear the entire house in one shot. Works well for estates with antiques, art, jewelry, or collectibles worth maximizing individually.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-item">
            <h4>Donation and junk removal</h4>
            <p>For estates where the items don't have significant resale value, donating to local charities and hiring a cleanout crew is often the fastest path. It won't generate revenue, but it clears the home quickly and donations may have tax benefits for the estate.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-item">
            <h4>DIY sale</h4>
            <p>Families can run their own estate sale, but it requires significant time and effort: pricing, organizing, advertising, staffing the sale, and handling the cleanup afterward. It works best for smaller estates where family members have the capacity and the items don't require expert pricing.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2>How to Find a Reputable Estate Sale Company in DFW</h2>
        <p>Not all estate sale companies are equal. The DFW market has a number of established operators, but the quality varies. Here's what to look for when evaluating companies.</p>

        <div class="process-steps">
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Step 1</div>
            <h4>Ask for references and look at past sales</h4>
            <p>Most reputable companies list their past sales on their website or on platforms like EstateSales.net. Look at how their sales are organized, how items are priced, and how they present the inventory. A well-run company's past sales will look professional and thorough.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Step 2</div>
            <h4>Understand their commission structure</h4>
            <p>Ask upfront what percentage they take, what minimum they require, and whether there are any additional fees for cleanup, advertising, or unsold items. A company that isn't transparent about this is a red flag.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Step 3</div>
            <h4>Ask about their process for valuable items</h4>
            <p>If the estate includes jewelry, art, antiques, coins, or firearms, ask how they handle appraisals and pricing for those items. Some companies have specialists; others will undervalue things out of unfamiliarity.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Step 4</div>
            <h4>Get a contract</h4>
            <p>Everything should be in writing before they set foot in the home. The contract should spell out the commission rate, what happens to unsold items, when you get paid, and what their cleanup responsibilities are after the sale.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Step 5</div>
            <h4>Check reviews independently</h4>
            <p>Google reviews, Yelp, and EstateSales.net all have customer feedback. Look for patterns across reviews, not just the overall star rating. Pay attention to comments about honesty, communication, and how disputes were handled.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2>Before the Estate Sale: What to Do First</h2>
        <p>Before any sale company comes in, the family should go through the home and remove anything they want to keep. Once items are priced and the sale begins, it can be difficult to pull things back. Do a thorough walk-through with all relevant heirs before you hand the home over to the sale company.</p>
        <p>Also check for and secure any important documents: wills, deeds, financial statements, insurance policies, passports, and social security cards. These should never end up in a sale box.</p>
        <p>If you're unsure whether something has significant value, such as art, jewelry, coins, or vintage items, get an independent appraisal before the sale company prices it. A general estate sale company may not specialize in these categories and could undervalue them.</p>

        <h2>What Happens to Items That Don't Sell?</h2>
        <p>Ask about this before you sign any contract. Options typically include donating remaining items to charity, having a buyout company purchase the remainder at a bulk rate, scheduling an additional clearance day with reduced prices, or arranging for a junk removal company to haul everything away.</p>
        <p>Some estate sale companies handle the full cleanout as part of their service. Others leave unsold items for the family to deal with. Know which situation you're signing up for before the sale begins.</p>

        <h2>Estate Sale Timing and the Property Sale</h2>
        <p>If you're planning to sell the house after the estate sale, the timing matters. A cleared, clean home photographs and shows much better than one still full of belongings. Coordinating the estate sale, any repairs or cleaning, and the property listing in the right sequence can make a meaningful difference in how the home presents to buyers and what it ultimately sells for.</p>
        <p>This is an area where having a real estate resource involved early helps. We work with families to coordinate the full sequence from estate sale through property closing and can point you toward trusted vendors in DFW for each stage of the process.</p>

        <div class="callout-box">
          <h4>Need a trusted estate sale company or cleanout crew in DFW?</h4>
          <p>We maintain a list of vetted local vendors, including estate sale companies, cleanout crews, estate attorneys, and other professionals we've worked with and trust. Reach out to us directly and we'll point you in the right direction based on your specific situation.</p>
        </div>

        <h2>If the Home Is Being Sold As-Is</h2>
        <p>Not every situation calls for a full estate sale. If you're selling the property to a cash buyer and the timeline is tight, a full cleanout may not be necessary. Many cash buyers, including us, will purchase a home with contents still inside. You take what you want and leave the rest. This can be the right call when the estate is in probate, the timeline is short, or the family simply doesn't have the bandwidth to manage a full sale process.</p>
        <p>It's worth having a conversation about what makes sense for your specific situation before committing to any particular path.</p>

